  "grammar_notes": [
    {
      "title": "Talking About the Past ('yakagadzikwa')",
      "content": "The verb 'yakagadzikwa' expresses a completed past action. Shona has two main ways to talk about the past: 1) For actions that happened earlier today or very recently, combine the short subject prefix ('nda-' = I, 'wa-' = you, 'a-' = he/she, 'ta-' = we, 'va-' = they) directly with the verb stem (e.g., 'ndamuka' = 'I woke up today', 'wabudirira' = 'you have succeeded'). 2) For events that happened yesterday or further in the past, insert '-ka-' after the subject prefix (e.g., 'takatenga' = 'we bought', 'vakaenda' = 'they went', 'akadanidzira' = 'he shouted').",
      "rule": "Recent Today Past: [nda- / wa- / ta- / va-] + verb | Earlier Past: [ndaka- / waka- / taka- / vaka-] + verb"
    },
    {
      "title": "Talking About Location: 'pa-', 'ku-', and 'mu-' ('pasi')",
      "content": "Look at how location is expressed with 'pasi'. Instead of separate prepositions like 'on', 'to', or 'in', Shona attaches one of three location prefixes directly to the front of a noun:\n• 'pa-' means 'at' or 'on' a specific point or surface (e.g., 'patafura' = on the table, 'pamusika' = at the market, 'pasi' = on the ground).\n• 'ku-' means 'to', 'from', or 'at' a general destination (e.g., 'kuchikoro' = to school, 'kumusha' = to the rural home, 'kubasa' = at/to work).\n• 'mu-' means 'inside' an enclosed space (e.g., 'mumba' = inside the house, 'muguta' = in the city, 'mumugwagwa' = in the road).",
      "rule": "pa- = on / at a point (patafura) | ku- = to / at a place (kuchikoro) | mu- = inside (mumba)"
    },
    {
      "title": "Possessive Pronouns: Saying 'My', 'Your', and 'Our' ('Shangu')",
      "content": "In Shona, possessive words like 'Shangu' ('my/your/our/their') always follow the noun they belong to! The six core possessive stems are '-ngu' (my), '-ko' (your), '-ke' (his/her/its), '-du' (our), '-nyu' (your plural/polite), and '-vo' (their). A short linking sound ('wa-', 'ya-', 'ra-', 'cha-', 'dza-', 'ma-') attaches to the front of the stem to match the noun: for example, 'shamwari yangu' (my friend), 'munda wako' (your field), 'mabhuku edu' (our books), and 'kubudirira kwenyu' (your success).",
      "rule": "Noun + [Linking Prefix + -ngu (my) / -ko (your) / -ke (his/her) / -du (our) / -nyu (your polite) / -vo (their)]"
    }
  ],
  "cultural_notes": [
    {
      "title": "Traditional Herbal Roots ('Midzi') & Landscaped Rockery Gardens",
      "content": "In Zimbabwean ethnobotany, dried barks and roots ('midzi') from indigenous trees are valued traditional remedies, while suburban gardens often showcase natural granite rockeries ('matombo akaita kamurwi') surrounded by flowers.",
      "rule": null
    }
  ],
